WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:08.469
Dr. Arnn: Welcome to the 163rd commencement
exercises of Hillsdale College. My name is

00:00:08.469 --> 00:00:14.360
Larry Arnn. We have here today the chairman
of the board, Bill Broadbeck, the vice chairman

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.770
of the board, Pat Sajak. Other members of
the board including Cleves Delp, whose daughter

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:24.150
is graduating today, and all of you ladies
and gentleman welcome. That was bishop and

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:30.779
Chaplain Peter Beckwith, and Provost David
Whalen that you just heard from. I am the

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:38.129
president of the college. As we meet here
today, the world is changing very fast. This

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:44.129
reinforces the view that changes the only
constant, that development replaces nature,

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:54.400
but history replaces God. Yet the ceremony
has been done 162 times before, every time

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.760
like this one.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.830
The form of this ceremony is as old as the
first universities, in the 11th and 12th centuries.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:10.000
For almost 1000 years then, people have come
here to do what we’re going to do today.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.970
It is not accidental. It is chosen, intended,
and when we human beings choose and intend

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:22.780
anything serious for a long time, it is likely,
we think, that it is blessed by something

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:31.930
beyond history and change. We think it is
blessed by nature and by providence. We make

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:39.850
the claim here today, so it has been made
for 1000 years. What is the purpose of the

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:49.990
ceremony? There are 325 of them sitting there.
We’re going to pronounce them fit and ready

00:01:49.990 --> 00:02:00.890
to live fully human lives in intellect and
character, as nature intended.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:07.930
We think that means they are intended to be
free. Free to speak their minds. Free to worship

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:14.420
their maker before whom they must stand. Free
to learn the things both moral and intellectual,

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:21.370
that are excellent and worthy. Free to pursue
those things according to their own conscience.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:27.670
Free to seek the happiness that can only be
found in these activities. Free and required

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:32.080
to stand up to the pleasures and the pains
that will tempt them to do what they ought

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:39.800
not. Free to prepare themselves when they
are young to do that. We say today, before

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:44.700
the day is out, that they have done that,
and present them to the world with that claim

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.900
from us.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:54.190
The ceremony takes its form from this nature,
and also it takes its form from the need to

00:02:54.190 --> 00:03:01.540
recognize everyone who has made it happen.
There are three to be recognized. Begin with

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:08.530
the parents of these seniors. We take great
pride here in our relationship with the parents

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:14.750
who send their children to us. We return them
back to them as adults. They come to us in

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:19.630
droves on parents’ weekend. How many are
here today? How many parents are here today?

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:28.050
Would you stand up for a minute please?

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:44.129
Do you feel completed or exhausted? We’re
more like you now than we were 4 years ago

00:03:44.129 --> 00:03:51.430
when you showed up here, because we feel exhausted
now too by these people. Parents weekend,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:58.989
our faculty, who sit there, I will say a word
about them in a minute. They take hours, twice

00:03:58.989 --> 00:04:05.190
a year, to meet with every parent that wants
to meet with them, any parent. They tend to

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:09.319
be the parents of the students they have in
class, but I know they answer emails from

00:04:09.319 --> 00:04:18.190
all of you whenever you send them. I do too.
We’re, by our preparedness to forego certain

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:21.819
revenues that come from Washington, DC, we’re
able to talk to you about your student more

00:04:21.819 --> 00:04:28.240
freely than other places are. We find a partnership
with you. The word college means partnership.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.419
These three groups that I’m naming, they
are the component parts of the partnership.

00:04:32.419 --> 00:04:37.300
Whatever has happened that has led us to this
place today, we’ve done that together. We

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:43.229
want you to know that we remember that. We’re
proud of you and grateful to know you. I love

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:48.449
to say, and I mean it when I say it, people
always come up to me and they say, “I want

00:04:48.449 --> 00:04:53.240
to thank you for what you’ve done for my
son and my daughter.” I always say, “Couldn’t

00:04:53.240 --> 00:05:00.139
break them with a stick, after you’ve done
what you’ve done.” Then there are the

00:05:00.139 --> 00:05:06.930
faculty. Four of them are retiring today.
Ellen Justice Templeton, a French teacher,

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:13.639
who graduated from the college, has taught
here for 44 years. Dan Sendall, not here today,

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:18.969
literature teacher, taught here for 30 years.
Brian Zeller, a math teacher, has taught here

00:05:18.969 --> 00:05:25.499
for 25 years. Will Morrisey, a politics teacher,
came here the same year I did, been here 15

00:05:25.499 --> 00:05:26.699
years.

00:05:26.699 --> 00:05:33.969
They stand for the others, 160 of them maybe.
I can’t remember how many we have. All precious,

00:05:33.969 --> 00:05:41.529
and think what kind of people they are. They’ve
all got 30 years of serious education. They’ve

00:05:41.529 --> 00:05:47.490
all given their lives to know something that’s
not very ruminative to know. Then they take

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:52.499
all that knowledge and they’re all used,
by the way, to being the smartest people around,

00:05:52.499 --> 00:05:58.300
in that academic way of being smart, which
means patient with the details but also able

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:03.210
to grasp some big thing that’s implicit
in the details. They all have minds like that.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.300
That’s why they’re such a joy to know.
That’s why you learn. Whenever you’re

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:13.529
around them, you can’t help it. Then they
take these 18-year-olds, now 21-year-olds,

00:06:13.529 --> 00:06:21.050
who show up and they give them all that. That’s
their calling. That’s their life’s work.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:27.180
They take enormous satisfaction from that.
They are irreplaceable and the best thing

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:33.080
in the college, and I thank them.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:46.899
Then the third group that have made all this
possible are of course the seniors themselves.

00:06:46.899 --> 00:06:53.659
Human beings are the kind of thing that are
responsible for their own making. They contribute

00:06:53.659 --> 00:06:59.509
to it. The philosopher says they hear a little
voice all the time, telling them what they

00:06:59.509 --> 00:07:05.270
should do. Then they hear other things too.
Something threatens pain. Something promises

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:11.479
pleasure. Something promises an easy way.
Something promises they can have something

00:07:11.479 --> 00:07:18.089
that really somebody else should have. They
hear both those voices all the time. What

00:07:18.089 --> 00:07:23.490
they become depends on which one they listen
to. Most of us listen to the better one most

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:28.349
of the time. You know, you don’t get a serious
education when you’re 21-years-old, unless

00:07:28.349 --> 00:07:31.580
you do it an overwhelming majority of the
time.

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:35.729
There’s a reason why we leave our backpacks
around here all the time and nobody steals

00:07:35.729 --> 00:07:44.990
them. The reason is represented by these seniors
right here and now. What are they like? A

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:50.469
few months ago Paul Rahe, Professor Rahe, sent
Loray Ferguson to see me. I hadn’t met her

00:07:50.469 --> 00:07:57.809
before. She’s in this class. She’s won
a national Greek translation competition.

00:07:57.809 --> 00:08:02.569
We’re the best at Greek and Latin translation
in the world. By the way, this senior class

00:08:02.569 --> 00:08:06.849
… Actually there aren’t that many seniors
on the women’s track team, but women’s

00:08:06.849 --> 00:08:11.800
track team comes second in the country. The
classics competition teams in Greek and Latin,

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:16.309
they typically come first in the country.
It means it’s a flexible college. We have

00:08:16.309 --> 00:08:20.899
some range in us here. Also the music is really
good here.

00:08:20.899 --> 00:08:27.129
Loray, she’s a violinist and she’s classics
translator, and I get in this conversation

00:08:27.129 --> 00:08:32.200
with her. She wants to talk about … She
wants to write about and learn about morality

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:37.720
in the classical world, and morality in the
Christian world, early Christian world. I

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:42.330
know a little about that because one of my
teachers wrote about that. I start talking

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:48.390
to her ... Somebody who has some graduate
education in those things. I can’t say anything

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:56.010
she hadn’t thought of. Then there’s Joshua
Benjamins, who wins national Latin competitions.

00:08:56.010 --> 00:09:01.410
Then there’s Garrett West off to Yale. There’s
Ashley Wright off to Oxford. There’s Sam

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:07.760
Ryskamp, the outstanding senior man, fine
and smart. Shelly Peters, second in her family

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:16.360
to be the outstanding senior woman. There’s
our stinking resented valedictorian Matthew

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:33.690
Sullivan, who has defied me and outfoxed our
faculty, and graduated 6th in 27 years with

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:38.480
a 4.0.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:50.240
If he weren’t shorter than I, I would have
kicked him when I saw him earlier today. Then

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:56.310
there’s the salutatorian, Bailey Lindner,
who is about to go off and take the accounting

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:03.270
exam and overcome what happened last year,
when one of the 60 best national scores on

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:08.210
the CPA exam was a Hillsdale College student.
She’s promised to do better than that, just

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:25.090
so Sullivan doesn’t get all the praise around
here. It’s a sad thing. One of the finest

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:32.050
young women any of us ever met, was killed
a year ago, August, in a car accident, over

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:36.430
by Monroe, Michigan, and would have been a
member of this class. We should remember her

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:47.280
for just a second today. Her name was Brittany
Aimes.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:54.780
I would say to this class that I’ve never
seen someone so crowded at the top. We’re

00:10:54.780 --> 00:11:00.610
so blessed here at Hillsdale. The kids are
smart. Things go well. They go on to very

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:08.800
great things. This year it’s hard to name
them all, that do something splendid in character

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:17.040
and mind. Their consistent excellence is a
marvel to me. I ask everybody to congratulate

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.589
them on their splendid record here.

